msgid ""
"[[quarkus-vertx-http-config-group-auth-runtime-config_quarkus-http-auth-form-new-cookie-interval]]`link:#quarkus-vertx-http-config-group-auth-runtime-config_quarkus-http-auth-form-new-cookie-interval[quarkus.http.auth.form.new-cookie-interval]`\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"[.description]\n"
"--\n"
"How old a cookie can get before it will be replaced with a new cookie with an updated timeout, also referred to as \"renewal-timeout\". Note that smaller values will result in slightly more server load (as new encrypted cookies will be generated more often); however, larger values affect the inactivity timeout because the timeout is set when a cookie is generated. For example if this is set to 10 minutes, and the inactivity timeout is 30m, if a user's last request is when the cookie is 9m old then the actual timeout will happen 21m after the last request because the timeout is only refreshed when a new cookie is generated. That is, no timeout is tracked on the server side; the timestamp is encoded and encrypted in the cookie itself, and it is decrypted and parsed with each request.\n"
"\n"
"Environment variable: env_var_with_copy_button:+++QUARKUS_HTTP_AUTH_FORM_NEW_COOKIE_INTERVAL+++[]\n"
"--"
msgstr "`link:#quarkus-vertx-http-config-group-auth-runtime-config_quarkus-http-auth-form-new-cookie-interval[quarkus.http.auth.form.new-cookie-interval]`"

#. mt: deepl
#, fuzzy
msgid "[[quarkus-vertx-http-config-group-auth-runtime-config_quarkus-http-auth-form-new-cookie-interval]]`link:#quarkus-vertx-http-config-group-auth-runtime-config_quarkus-http-auth-form-new-cookie-interval[quarkus.http.auth.form.new-cookie-interval]`"
msgstr "`link:#quarkus-vertx-http-config-group-auth-runtime-config_quarkus-http-auth-form-new-cookie-interval[quarkus.http.auth.form.new-cookie-interval]`"

#. mt: deepl
#, fuzzy
msgid "How old a cookie can get before it will be replaced with a new cookie with an updated timeout, also referred to as \"renewal-timeout\". Note that smaller values will result in slightly more server load (as new encrypted cookies will be generated more often); however, larger values affect the inactivity timeout because the timeout is set when a cookie is generated. For example if this is set to 10 minutes, and the inactivity timeout is 30m, if a user's last request is when the cookie is 9m old then the actual timeout will happen 21m after the last request because the timeout is only refreshed when a new cookie is generated. That is, no timeout is tracked on the server side; the timestamp is encoded and encrypted in the cookie itself, and it is decrypted and parsed with each request."
msgstr "El tiempo que puede permanecer una cookie antes de que sea sustituida por una nueva con un tiempo de caducidad actualizado, también denominado «tiempo de caducidad de renovación». Tenga en cuenta que los valores más bajos supondrán una carga ligeramente mayor para el servidor (ya que se generarán nuevas cookies cifradas con mayor frecuencia); sin embargo, los valores más altos afectan al tiempo de espera por inactividad, ya que este se establece en el momento en que se genera la cookie. Por ejemplo, si se establece en 10 minutos y el tiempo de espera por inactividad es de 30 minutos, si la última solicitud de un usuario se produce cuando la cookie tiene 9 minutos de antigüedad, el tiempo de espera real se alcanzará 21 minutos después de la última solicitud, ya que el tiempo de espera solo se actualiza cuando se genera una nueva cookie. Es decir, no se realiza ningún seguimiento del tiempo de espera en el lado del servidor; la marca de tiempo se codifica y se cifra en la propia cookie, y se descifra y se analiza con cada solicitud."

